  "content": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "Beyond linear equations, the EST II tests rational expressions, absolute value equations, and advanced polynomial manipulation.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "cards",
        "items": [
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Rational expression",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\dfrac{P(x)}{Q(x)},\\ Q\\neq 0 \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Absolute value",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( |x-a|=b \\Rightarrow x=a\\pm b \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Remainder theorem",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( f(k) = \\) remainder of \\( f\\div(x-k) \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Factor theorem",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( f(k)=0 \\Leftrightarrow (x-k)\\mid f(x) \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 1.A",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "Simplify \\( \\dfrac{x^2-9}{x^2-x-6} \\). \\[ \\frac{(x-3)(x+3)}{(x-3)(x+2)}=\\frac{x+3}{x+2},\\quad x\\neq 3. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "<h3>Systems and inequalities</h3>Graphical solutions of systems of inequalities define a feasible region. To find vertices, solve pairs of boundary lines simultaneously. EST II questions may ask for the maximum or minimum of a linear expression over the feasible region — an introduction to linear programming.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "def",
        "label": {
          "en": "● Advanced Algebra",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<strong>Polynomial long division:</strong> divide \\(p(x)\\) by \\(d(x)\\) to get quotient \\(q(x)\\) and remainder \\(r(x)\\). <strong>Rational equations:</strong> multiply through by LCD, then solve, checking for extraneous solutions. <strong>Logarithmic equations:</strong> use log laws to combine terms, then convert to exponential form.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example — Rational equation with extraneous solution",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "Solve \\(\\dfrac{x}{x-2} + \\dfrac{1}{x+2} = \\dfrac{8}{x^2-4}\\). <p>LCD \\(= (x-2)(x+2)\\). Multiply through: \\(x(x+2)+(x-2) = 8\\).</p><p>\\(x^2+2x+x-2 = 8 \\Rightarrow x^2+3x-10 = 0 \\Rightarrow (x+5)(x-2)=0\\).</p><p>\\(x=2\\) is extraneous (denominator = 0). Solution: \\(x = -5\\).</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "Solve \\(\\log_2(x+4) - \\log_2 x = 3\\).",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "\\(\\log_2\\dfrac{x+4}{x} = 3 \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{x+4}{x} = 8 \\Rightarrow x+4 = 8x \\Rightarrow x = \\dfrac{4}{7}\\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      }
    ]
  },
